WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:10.160
The Gobi desert is the largest in Asia, spanning&nbsp;
1000 miles or 1600 km. Tucked away in the part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:16.480
the desert in the northwestern Chinese province of&nbsp;
Gansu is something that looks out of this world. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:23.200
mock Mars base opened in 2019. Visitors experience&nbsp;
what life is like on a real space mission.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:29.840
This is not just for show. This is a glimpse of&nbsp;
Beijing's bid to become the next space superpower.

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:41.840
During the 70th anniversary of the People's&nbsp;
Republic of China in 2019. President Xi Jinping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:48.000
said no force can stop China's progress. He&nbsp;
wasn't just talking about dominance on earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.840
but in space. There is a lot of catching up to do.&nbsp;
The Soviet Union sent the first person to space in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:01:01.040
April 1961, cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin. American Alan&nbsp;
Shepard followed the next month. Meanwhile, China&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:07.200
took four more decades to get there. The first&nbsp;
Chinese astronaut to go was Yang Liwei in 2003.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:13.040
However, Beijing believes it can conquer space&nbsp;
- just look at the name it gives its rockets:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:18.640
Long March. The long march refers to the famous&nbsp;
military tactic by the communists when they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:23.680
nearly defeated by the nationalists during the&nbsp;
Chinese civil war under the leadership of Mao&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:31.440
Zedong. The communists retreated on a brutal&nbsp;
6,000-mile (10,000-km) trek starting in 1934.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:37.280
They used that escape to regroup, rebuild,&nbsp;
and eventually win the war. In the same way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:42.480
victory in space could come after much struggle&nbsp;
and it's already gotten a small taste of it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:49.440
In December 2018, China went where no&nbsp;
human or machine had ever gone before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:55.920
sending the Chang'e 4 lander and rover to the far&nbsp;
side of the moon and this is simply the beginning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:00.800
China aims to build a research station at the&nbsp;
moon's southern pole - a region of particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:05.760
interest because water is likely to exist frozen&nbsp;
in the craters. That's also where it wants to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:11.920
send astronauts in a decade and it might not be&nbsp;
alone. NASA has contracted commercial companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:17.520
to help them return to the moon. SpaceX, Blue&nbsp;
Origin, and a newcomer called Dynetics are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:22.880
developing lunar landing systems for the agency.&nbsp;
The Chinese government has also been encouraging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:29.280
the growth of a commercial space sector. July&nbsp;
2019, the firm i-Space sent the first privately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:34.800
funded Chinese rocket into orbit. Hyperbola-&nbsp;
1 launched a radio satellite and several small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:40.400
payloads. China eventually envisions private&nbsp;
companies launching a constellation of satellites&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:44.960
offering services like high-speed internet,&nbsp;
similar to what SpaceX is doing right now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:51.280
Elon Musk's company has been launching&nbsp;
Starlink satellites using the Falcon 9,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:55.040
a partially reusable rocket because&nbsp;
the booster lands back on Earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.040 --> 00:03:00.400
Reusing rockets to drastically bring down costs&nbsp;
is what separates SpaceX from its competition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:07.840
Many Chinese companies are starting to test&nbsp;
out reusability as well. In August 2019, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:13.040
startup LinkSpace launched a rocket that hovered&nbsp;
300 meters in the air before coming back down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:19.200
It might sound like a small feat considering what&nbsp;
SpaceX has achieved but it's a big first step.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:24.960
A bigger step is the national space agency's&nbsp;
plan to operate a reusable Long March rocket.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:30.720
Version 8 is supposedly the answer to the Falcon&nbsp;
9. It, too, would land vertically - notably with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:35.600
its side boosters intact. The test flight&nbsp;
is believed to be scheduled for this year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:39.520
although it's possible we may not know until&nbsp;
afterward because much of what happens in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:45.040
country is shrouded in mystery. For example,&nbsp;
in September, Chinese state media said it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:50.880
had launched a reusable experimental spacecraft&nbsp;
without saying much more. A story from three years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:56.800
ago did refer to a launch in 2020 that would fly&nbsp;
into the sky like an aircraft - bringing to mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:02.080
Boeing's space plane used by the U.S. Air Force&nbsp;
for covert missions. It takes off vertically and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:07.920
lands horizontally on a runway. Even though a&nbsp;
lot is kept under wraps, one thing is clear:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:12.480
China will not be going to the International&nbsp;
Space Station since U.S. Congress banned them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:16.720
due to security concerns. So what do you&nbsp;
do when you're not welcome at the ISS?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:22.800
You build your own. The Chinese space&nbsp;
station is slated to be completed by 2022.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.440
Beijing has already inked a deal for European&nbsp;
astronauts to visit - that's NASA's partner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:34.160
the European Space Agency. Besides the station and&nbsp;
the moon, China's next ambition is to get to Mars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:40.880
In July, the Tianwen-1 rover headed on a path to&nbsp;
the red planet. The expected arrival in February&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:47.200
next year coincides with NASA's Perseverance rover&nbsp;
landing on Mars. The super heavy lift Long March 5&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:52.160
launched the chinese rover. It's the country's&nbsp;
biggest rocket so far yet it will be small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:57.440
in comparison to the Long March 9 currently&nbsp;
under development, which will be used for crewed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:03.600
missions to deep space starting around 2030. It's&nbsp;
similar in size to NASA's Space Launch System for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:09.440
missions beyond Earth's orbit with bigger versions&nbsp;
to come. SpaceX's Starship towers over them all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:16.000
at 120 meters but Long March's carrying capacity&nbsp;
is said to be similar to Starship's - reportedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:22.480
holding 140 metric tons to low Earth orbit.&nbsp;
Starship will carry over 100 metric tons and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:28.480
up to 150 MTU for optimized versions. There is&nbsp;
a big difference between the two: the Long March&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:35.600
9 is not reusable whereas Starship will be fully&nbsp;
reusable, which makes SpaceX untouchable for now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:40.960
But China is making inroads to the point where&nbsp;
other countries are trying not to get left behind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:45.600
Japan and India have agreed to work together&nbsp;
on missions to the moon in order to counter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:50.480
China's presence. That doesn't mean China&nbsp;
will become a space superpower overnight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:56.560
It still has a lot to learn. In September,&nbsp;
video posted on chinese social media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:02.080
apparently showed the booster of a Long March 4B&nbsp;
rocket crashing and narrowly missing a school in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:08.240
the northwestern Chinese province of Shanxi. So&nbsp;
more time is needed to perfect the technology.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:11.600
If there's anything we've learned&nbsp;
about China is that it learns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:15.840
fast - like in manufacturing - where it worked&nbsp;
efficiently and raced ahead of the pack.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:23.280
Despite the long road or long march ahead,&nbsp;
when China is set on conquering an industry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:28.080
there appears to be no stopping them.&nbsp;
Hey guys, thanks so much for watching,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:33.280
I'm Cindy Pom. So do you think China will ever&nbsp;
catch up with other companies, including SpaceX?&nbsp;&nbsp;

